WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.209
This is the technical deep
dive for Book 02 of the

2
00:00:03.209 --> 00:00:07.000
Federal Modernization Workbook,
Identity and Directory.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.197
It's for the architects and
engineers who will actually

4
00:00:10.197 --> 00:00:13.463
run the move from Active
Directory to Microsoft Entra ID.

5
00:00:13.463 --> 00:00:16.536
It comes in two parts of about
twenty-five minutes each,

6
00:00:16.536 --> 00:00:18.926
and four modules you can watch separately.

7
00:00:18.926 --> 00:00:20.723
Part 1 is assessing the forest:

8
00:00:20.723 --> 00:00:24.824
first what a standard account can
learn without any elevated access,

9
00:00:24.824 --> 00:00:29.212
then the full privileged assessment
and the evidence pipeline behind it.

10
00:00:29.212 --> 00:00:34.016
Part 2 is building the Entra ID target
and migrating onto it: the sync, sign-in,

11
00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:38.976
group, service account, privileged
access and Conditional Access decisions,

12
00:00:38.976 --> 00:00:41.765
then governance, the
eleven-phase playbook,

13
00:00:41.765 --> 00:00:46.337
and a reference deployment that shows
the whole sequence on one estate.

14
00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:50.087
The slides show tables,
flows and decision matrices.

15
00:00:50.087 --> 00:00:52.781
The code lives in the
chapters and appendices;

16
00:00:52.781 --> 00:00:55.840
I'll point you to it rather
than put it on screen.

17
00:00:56.590 --> 00:00:57.778
Why this matters.

18
00:00:57.778 --> 00:01:02.903
Federal policy moved the access decision
off the network and onto identity.

19
00:01:02.903 --> 00:01:07.020
OMB M-22-09 requires
centralized identity management,

20
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:12.316
phishing-resistant multifactor authentication
for staff, contractors and partners,

21
00:01:12.316 --> 00:01:14.522
and at least one device-level signal,

22
00:01:14.522 --> 00:01:18.714
and it says users should log into
applications rather than networks.

23
00:01:18.714 --> 00:01:25.421
NIST SP 800-207 grants no implicit
trust from network location:

24
00:01:25.421 --> 00:01:28.615
access is decided per
session by dynamic policy.

25
00:01:28.615 --> 00:01:34.011
And CISA's Zero Trust Maturity Model makes
identity the first of its five pillars,

26
00:01:34.011 --> 00:01:37.027
validated continuously
at the optimal level.

27
00:01:37.027 --> 00:01:41.277
So the identity provider is the policy
decision point for the whole estate.

28
00:01:41.277 --> 00:01:43.682
Whatever it believes about a person,

29
00:01:43.682 --> 00:01:48.252
a group or a device is what gets
enforced, everywhere, at machine speed.

30
00:01:48.252 --> 00:01:51.086
Microsoft isn't retiring Active Directory;

31
00:01:51.086 --> 00:01:54.077
it says it continues to
support and enhance it.

32
00:01:54.077 --> 00:01:57.116
The forcing comes from the
direction of its investment.

33
00:01:57.116 --> 00:01:59.548
Cloud Sync is replacing Connect Sync,

34
00:01:59.548 --> 00:02:03.628
which retires once Cloud Sync
reaches full functional parity.

35
00:02:03.628 --> 00:02:06.519
Microsoft recommends moving from AD FS to

36
00:02:06.519 --> 00:02:09.740
cloud authentication
with password hash sync.

37
00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:14.691
All versions of NTLM were
deprecated in June 2024,

38
00:02:14.691 --> 00:02:21.090
and NTLMv1 is removed in Windows
11 24H2 and Windows Server 2025.

39
00:02:21.090 --> 00:02:24.429
And since January 2026
you can convert a synced

40
00:02:24.429 --> 00:02:27.340
user to cloud-managed,
one object at a time.

41
00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.402
The trap is the lift and shift.

42
00:02:29.402 --> 00:02:32.940
Synchronize the forest as it
stands, and stale accounts,

43
00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:37.710
undocumented trusts and synced privileged
accounts arrive in the tenant,

44
00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:40.787
while the delegation and
policy intent that the

45
00:02:40.787 --> 00:02:44.770
organizational unit (OU) tree
carried has nowhere to land.

46
00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.595
What has to move.

47
00:02:46.595 --> 00:02:52.320
Chapters 02 through 04 make two points
that drive everything in this session.

48
00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.932
First, placement becomes attributes.

49
00:02:54.932 --> 00:02:58.442
In Active Directory, an object's
position in the forest,

50
00:02:58.442 --> 00:03:03.433
domain and OU tree decides who administers
it and which Group Policy applies.

51
00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:06.632
Move a user to another OU
and its administrators,

52
00:03:06.632 --> 00:03:12.232
inherited policies and often its access
all change without anyone editing the user.

53
00:03:12.232 --> 00:03:14.057
Entra ID has no tree.

54
00:03:14.057 --> 00:03:17.227
It governs objects by what
they carry: attributes,

55
00:03:17.227 --> 00:03:20.707
memberships, role assignments
and policy conditions.

56
00:03:20.707 --> 00:03:25.557
Two limits decide how far the usual
answer, administrative units, gets you.

57
00:03:25.557 --> 00:03:30.757
Administrative units can't nest, so an
OU tree becomes a flat set of units.

58
00:03:30.757 --> 00:03:33.396
An object can belong to
at most thirty of them.

59
00:03:33.396 --> 00:03:37.854
And for synced users, Graph treats
the extension attributes as read-only,

60
00:03:37.854 --> 00:03:41.645
because their source of authority
is still Active Directory.

61
00:03:41.645 --> 00:03:44.268
So the values your rules
match on are written

62
00:03:44.268 --> 00:03:46.820
on-premises and arrive
with each sync cycle.

63
00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:50.245
Second, the surface is bigger
than users and groups.

64
00:03:50.245 --> 00:03:53.945
The program's inventory names
eleven dependency types.

65
00:03:53.945 --> 00:03:56.483
The first five are what
plans usually count.

66
00:03:56.483 --> 00:04:03.094
Types six through nine, DNS and DHCP, PKI,
RADIUS and the Network Policy Server,

67
00:04:03.094 --> 00:04:08.446
and LDAP-bound applications,
fail silently when AD goes away.

68
00:04:08.446 --> 00:04:12.669
Types ten and eleven, service
principal names and trusts,

69
00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:15.545
have no direct counterpart in Entra ID.

70
00:04:15.545 --> 00:04:17.607
Each dependency gets an adapter.

71
00:04:17.607 --> 00:04:23.546
The PKI, RADIUS and LDAP proxy adapters
are critical, because they fail silently.

72
00:04:23.546 --> 00:04:25.401
The sync engine finishes last,

73
00:04:25.401 --> 00:04:29.857
because it's what keeps AD in production
until everything else has moved.

74
00:04:29.857 --> 00:04:34.830
The rest of this session is how you find
all of that, rebuild it, and prove it.

75
00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:38.155
Module 1: read-only discovery.

76
00:04:38.155 --> 00:04:41.655
An assessment team rarely gets
privileged access on day one.

77
00:04:41.655 --> 00:04:47.017
Authorization paperwork, security review
and trust between teams all take time.

78
00:04:47.017 --> 00:04:49.602
The program spends that
window learning what

79
00:04:49.602 --> 00:04:52.118
the directory already
tells every employee.

80
00:04:52.118 --> 00:04:55.789
A standard domain account, with
no rights beyond the default,

81
00:04:55.789 --> 00:04:58.580
can read most of what
the migration plan needs.

82
00:04:58.580 --> 00:05:03.298
Starting there changes nothing in the
directory, applies least privilege,

83
00:05:03.298 --> 00:05:09.309
NIST SP 800-53 control AC-6,
to the assessment team itself,

84
00:05:09.309 --> 00:05:13.037
and turns the request for
elevated access into a list built

85
00:05:13.037 --> 00:05:17.210
from evidence instead of a
general request for Domain Admin.

86
00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.510
Here's what a standard
account typically reads.

87
00:05:20.510 --> 00:05:25.806
Forest and domain topology, domain
controllers, sites, subnets and site links are

88
00:05:25.806 --> 00:05:31.022
readable, which gives you scope, functional
levels and the shape of replication.

89
00:05:31.022 --> 00:05:34.508
The OU hierarchy and Group
Policy links are readable:

90
00:05:34.508 --> 00:05:38.310
that's the structure the
attribute design later replaces.

91
00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:41.685
GPOs and their settings
reports are usually readable,

92
00:05:41.685 --> 00:05:45.135
unless security filtering
restricts a particular GPO.

93
00:05:45.135 --> 00:05:49.260
Users, groups, computers and service
principal names are readable,

94
00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:54.060
which is your identity inventory and
the start of service account discovery.

95
00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:59.857
Certificate templates and enrollment
services sit in the configuration partition,

96
00:05:59.857 --> 00:06:02.960
so the AD CS dependency
surface is visible too.

97
00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:07.223
AD-integrated DNS zones are
often readable; test it.

98
00:06:07.223 --> 00:06:10.064
What you usually can't
see is replication health

99
00:06:10.064 --> 00:06:12.622
and DNS server settings
such as scavenging.

100
00:06:12.622 --> 00:06:14.801
Those need a right or server access,

101
00:06:14.801 --> 00:06:19.159
so they become a delegation item or
an administrator-provided export.

102
00:06:19.159 --> 00:06:21.010
Why is the default so broad?

103
00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:24.210
Read access is the sum of
several default grants.

104
00:06:24.210 --> 00:06:27.662
Authenticated Users receives
the general, public,

105
00:06:27.662 --> 00:06:31.772
personal and web information
property sets on user objects.

106
00:06:31.772 --> 00:06:36.755
Pre–Windows 2000 Compatible Access
receives list, read-all-properties

107
00:06:36.755 --> 00:06:41.822
and read-permissions rights on users,
groups and InetOrgPerson objects.

108
00:06:41.822 --> 00:06:46.094
Its membership depends on a choice
made when the domain was created:

109
00:06:46.094 --> 00:06:50.366
in the Windows 2000-and-later mode
it contains Authenticated Users;

110
00:06:50.366 --> 00:06:53.722
in the older compatible
mode, Everyone and Anonymous.

111
00:06:53.722 --> 00:06:58.544
So in most domains a standard account
reads most attributes of users and groups,

112
00:06:58.544 --> 00:07:01.685
but the exact result depends
on the domain's history.

113
00:07:01.685 --> 00:07:04.566
The first step is therefore
a pre-flight that tests

114
00:07:04.566 --> 00:07:07.448
what this account can
actually read in this forest.

115
00:07:07.448 --> 00:07:11.300
Every later export records
which access it relied on.

116
00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.825
The assessment report should never
claim coverage it doesn't have,

117
00:07:15.825 --> 00:07:19.462
so you need to know exactly where
the default read boundary sits.

118
00:07:19.462 --> 00:07:23.113
Four mechanisms set it, and
several are commonly misstated.

119
00:07:23.113 --> 00:07:25.200
Protected accounts and groups.

120
00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:30.367
The AdminSDHolder object holds template
permissions for Domain Admins,

121
00:07:30.367 --> 00:07:35.450
Enterprise Admins, Schema Admins, the
operator groups, krbtgt and others.

122
00:07:35.450 --> 00:07:39.064
A process called S D Prop
runs every sixty minutes by

123
00:07:39.064 --> 00:07:43.350
default on the PDC emulator and
resets those objects to match.

124
00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.288
It acts on current members only.

125
00:07:45.288 --> 00:07:48.181
An account removed from
a protected group keeps

126
00:07:48.181 --> 00:07:51.001
admin count set to one
unless someone clears it.

127
00:07:51.001 --> 00:07:54.495
So admin count lists accounts
that are or were privileged:

128
00:07:54.495 --> 00:07:58.062
a good clean-up list, not the
current privileged population.

129
00:07:58.062 --> 00:07:59.787
Confidential attributes.

130
00:07:59.787 --> 00:08:02.749
Setting bit seven, value
one hundred twenty-eight,

131
00:08:02.749 --> 00:08:06.649
of an attribute's search flags
means reading it needs the control

132
00:08:06.649 --> 00:08:10.476
access right as well, which by
default only administrators hold.

133
00:08:10.476 --> 00:08:14.908
You can still show where a capability
is deployed without seeing the secret:

134
00:08:14.908 --> 00:08:17.511
with Windows LAPS, the
presence and age of the

135
00:08:17.511 --> 00:08:20.325
password expiration
attribute shows coverage.

136
00:08:20.325 --> 00:08:24.504
Legacy Microsoft LAPS is
deprecated as of Windows 11 23H2,

137
00:08:24.504 --> 00:08:30.337
so a forest still on it has a finding before
the assessment even reaches identity.

138
00:08:30.337 --> 00:08:31.313
Secrets.

139
00:08:31.313 --> 00:08:35.413
Password hashes leave a domain
controller only through replication,

140
00:08:35.413 --> 00:08:40.362
which needs Replicating Directory Changes
and Replicating Directory Changes All.

141
00:08:40.362 --> 00:08:42.674
The assessment never requests those.

142
00:08:42.674 --> 00:08:45.978
But which accounts already hold
them is a key later check:

143
00:08:45.978 --> 00:08:50.687
any holder that isn't a domain controller
or the sync service account is a finding.

144
00:08:50.687 --> 00:08:52.338
Restricted containers.

145
00:08:52.338 --> 00:08:56.676
Deleted Objects is visible only
to System and Administrators.

146
00:08:56.676 --> 00:09:01.050
Restoring needs Reanimate Tombstone,
which an assessment never needs.

147
00:09:01.050 --> 00:09:04.498
Fine-grained password policies
belong to Domain Admins,

148
00:09:04.498 --> 00:09:07.725
so record them as not seen
rather than inferring them.

149
00:09:07.725 --> 00:09:10.930
Finally, report only measured coverage.

150
00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:16.135
Every gap read-only discovery couldn't
close gets recorded with four facts:

151
00:09:16.135 --> 00:09:19.943
what's missing, the narrowest right
or export that would close it,

152
00:09:19.943 --> 00:09:23.680
why the plan needs it, and whether
there's a read-only substitute.

153
00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:29.725
Replication health and domain controller
event logs come from an administrator-run

154
00:09:29.725 --> 00:09:34.918
summary, a log export, or SIEM access;
the event logs are where you see NTLM use.

155
00:09:34.918 --> 00:09:40.739
DNS server settings like aging and scavenging
come from a DNS administrator export,

156
00:09:40.739 --> 00:09:44.730
because zone reads cover
records but not server settings.

157
00:09:44.730 --> 00:09:48.609
The issued-certificate database
comes from a CA administrator,

158
00:09:48.609 --> 00:09:52.487
and tells you which certificates
must be reissued from a new PKI.

159
00:09:52.487 --> 00:09:56.805
Deleted Objects needs read on that
one container for one named account.

160
00:09:56.805 --> 00:10:00.767
Fine-grained password policies
need a Domain Admin export.

161
00:10:00.767 --> 00:10:04.677
And confidential attributes like
LAPS and BitLocker recovery

162
00:10:04.677 --> 00:10:08.143
data are never requested at
all: you report coverage only.

163
00:10:08.143 --> 00:10:12.451
The gap list becomes a short written
request, and it follows fixed rules,

164
00:10:12.451 --> 00:10:16.905
because the request is what makes the
least-privilege position credible.

165
00:10:16.905 --> 00:10:20.829
Read only: no write, create,
delete or permission rights,

166
00:10:20.829 --> 00:10:23.793
and never the rights that expose secrets.

167
00:10:23.793 --> 00:10:29.291
Specific: each line names the right, the
exact object path and the business reason;

168
00:10:29.291 --> 00:10:33.230
"Domain Admin for the assessment"
is not an acceptable line.

169
00:10:33.230 --> 00:10:37.517
Time-bound: each delegation
expires with the assessment window,

170
00:10:37.517 --> 00:10:40.105
and the account's expiry is set to match.

171
00:10:40.105 --> 00:10:44.618
One named, dedicated account with
no existing elevated memberships.

172
00:10:44.618 --> 00:10:48.217
And preferably an export: if
the directory team can run the

173
00:10:48.217 --> 00:10:51.892
report and hand over the output,
that beats granting a right.

174
00:10:51.892 --> 00:10:56.193
The directory team implements the
delegation with its own change process.

175
00:10:56.193 --> 00:10:57.305
Run it safely.

176
00:10:57.305 --> 00:11:02.054
Tell security operations first, because
broad LDAP enumeration looks like

177
00:11:02.054 --> 00:11:06.655
reconnaissance, and Defender for
Identity raises discovery alerts for it.

178
00:11:06.655 --> 00:11:12.406
The output is a map of the estate, so keep
it encrypted on a dedicated workstation,

179
00:11:12.406 --> 00:11:15.107
record a SHA-256 hash of every file in a

180
00:11:15.107 --> 00:11:19.030
manifest and disable the
account when you're done.

181
00:11:19.780 --> 00:11:22.293
Module 2: assessing the forest.

182
00:11:22.293 --> 00:11:25.167
Chapter 07 ends with a gap list.

183
00:11:25.167 --> 00:11:29.462
Chapter 08 fills it with the
privileged half of discovery:

184
00:11:29.462 --> 00:11:34.830
the configuration partition, ACLs on
OUs, every GPO's settings, DNS server

185
00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:38.587
configuration, the PKI
containers and CA settings,

186
00:11:38.587 --> 00:11:41.718
and the delegation flags on every account.

187
00:11:41.718 --> 00:11:44.620
The program's position is
that the assessment is

188
00:11:44.620 --> 00:11:47.668
an evidence-producing process,
not a set of scripts.

189
00:11:47.668 --> 00:11:52.921
Every run collects a fixed set of
artifacts into a fixed directory layout,

190
00:11:52.921 --> 00:11:56.451
writes a manifest with a
SHA-256 hash of every file,

191
00:11:56.451 --> 00:12:00.068
and commits the run to the
program's Git repository.

192
00:12:00.068 --> 00:12:04.528
Appendix B carries the complete
module, restored and corrected;

193
00:12:04.528 --> 00:12:08.010
Appendix C carries the
identity-side module.

194
00:12:08.760 --> 00:12:15.501
The full assessment covers eight areas,
run in a fixed order: forest topology,

195
00:12:15.501 --> 00:12:22.348
the OU hierarchy, Group Policy, DNS, AD CS,
computers, users and groups, and trusts.

196
00:12:22.348 --> 00:12:26.247
Most of the forest is readable
by any authenticated user.

197
00:12:26.247 --> 00:12:29.379
The parts that aren't are
the reason Chapter 08 exists,

198
00:12:29.379 --> 00:12:32.723
so each collector runs on the
narrowest right that works.

199
00:12:32.723 --> 00:12:37.575
Topology, computers, and users and
groups need only an authenticated user;

200
00:12:37.575 --> 00:12:40.758
delegation attributes
are readable by default,

201
00:12:40.758 --> 00:12:44.497
though recursive group expansion
is slow in big forests.

202
00:12:44.497 --> 00:12:49.168
The OU collector needs read permission
on the OUs' security descriptors,

203
00:12:49.168 --> 00:12:52.048
because some ACLs aren't
readable by default.

204
00:12:52.048 --> 00:12:54.998
Group Policy needs read
on the GPOs and SYSVOL,

205
00:12:54.998 --> 00:12:59.347
plus the backup right through
Group Policy Management delegation.

206
00:12:59.347 --> 00:13:04.523
DNS needs read on each DNS server,
delegated on the server object.

207
00:13:04.523 --> 00:13:09.710
AD CS needs read on the Public Key
Services containers and on each CA.

208
00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.827
And trusts: any user can list them,

209
00:13:11.827 --> 00:13:15.747
but verification needs domain
administrator credentials.

210
00:13:15.747 --> 00:13:18.697
Two rights the source design
asked for are refused.

211
00:13:18.697 --> 00:13:24.414
DnsAdmins: Microsoft treats it as privileged,
because its members control the DNS

212
00:13:24.414 --> 00:13:30.047
Server service, and Defender for Identity
flags non-privileged members as a risk.

213
00:13:30.047 --> 00:13:34.423
Grant read on the DNS server object
to a dedicated group instead.

214
00:13:34.423 --> 00:13:38.248
And standing Domain Admin:
no assessment run uses one.

215
00:13:38.248 --> 00:13:42.112
Trust verification is the only
step that needs those rights,

216
00:13:42.112 --> 00:13:45.435
and it runs separately under
just-in-time elevation.

217
00:13:45.435 --> 00:13:50.754
The host is a domain-joined Windows
Server 2025 or Windows 11 machine

218
00:13:50.754 --> 00:13:56.072
with the Remote Server Administration
Tools, at build 26100 or later.

219
00:13:56.072 --> 00:13:59.194
The account is a group
managed service account,

220
00:13:59.194 --> 00:14:03.597
with delegated read and nothing
more, so no password is ever stored.

221
00:14:03.597 --> 00:14:05.520
Two corrections from review:

222
00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:10.727
the AD CS administration module the source
required isn't used by any function,

223
00:14:10.727 --> 00:14:13.130
and the PSPKI module is now optional,

224
00:14:13.130 --> 00:14:17.100
because its publisher no
longer actively supports it.

225
00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.437
Three areas carry most
of the planning weight.

226
00:14:20.437 --> 00:14:21.838
Topology first.

227
00:14:21.838 --> 00:14:25.100
Active Directory has five
operations master roles.

228
00:14:25.100 --> 00:14:29.750
Two are forest-wide, the schema
master and the domain naming master.

229
00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:33.135
Three exist in every
domain: the PDC emulator,

230
00:14:33.135 --> 00:14:36.612
the RID master and the
infrastructure master.

231
00:14:36.612 --> 00:14:41.312
So a three-domain forest has eleven
role holders to record, not five.

232
00:14:41.312 --> 00:14:44.039
The PDC emulator in
the forest root is also

233
00:14:44.039 --> 00:14:47.000
the authoritative time
source for the forest.

234
00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:50.837
Record every functional level
and flag two conditions.

235
00:14:50.837 --> 00:14:56.607
A level below Windows Server 2016
blocks the domain controller refresh,

236
00:14:56.607 --> 00:15:02.375
because a Server 2025 controller can
join only a domain at 2016 or higher.

237
00:15:02.375 --> 00:15:04.025
And mixed domain levels.

238
00:15:04.025 --> 00:15:09.425
Server 2025 is the first new
functional level since 2016.

239
00:15:09.425 --> 00:15:13.305
Capture site links with cost and
interval, and the subnet map:

240
00:15:13.305 --> 00:15:17.650
an unmapped subnet sends clients
to arbitrary domain controllers.

241
00:15:17.650 --> 00:15:19.825
Second, the OU hierarchy.

242
00:15:19.825 --> 00:15:23.059
The goal is to capture what
the tree encodes so the

243
00:15:23.059 --> 00:15:26.450
attribute design reproduces
intent, not structure.

244
00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:29.748
For each OU, record placement
and size; its GPO links,

245
00:15:29.748 --> 00:15:34.851
preferably through Get-GP-Inheritance,
which returns link order, enforcement,

246
00:15:34.851 --> 00:15:40.269
enabled state and inheritance blocking;
its non-inherited access control entries,

247
00:15:40.269 --> 00:15:45.530
with built-in principals dropped, which
are the delegations someone made by hand

248
00:15:45.530 --> 00:15:50.712
and become the administrative unit
candidates; and a text rendering for review.

249
00:15:50.712 --> 00:15:55.562
Third, Group Policy, the largest
and most consequential artifact.

250
00:15:55.562 --> 00:16:00.163
Every live setting moves to Intune,
moves elsewhere, or retires.

251
00:16:00.163 --> 00:16:02.637
Sort GPOs into three groups.

252
00:16:02.637 --> 00:16:05.813
Unlinked or empty ones
are retirement candidates.

253
00:16:05.813 --> 00:16:08.719
Overlapping settings
must be resolved first,

254
00:16:08.719 --> 00:16:12.313
because Intune's migration
refuses conflicting values.

255
00:16:12.313 --> 00:16:14.088
Live settings need a decision.

256
00:16:14.088 --> 00:16:18.748
One correction: the source searched
only OUs and the domain root,

257
00:16:18.748 --> 00:16:21.387
so site-linked GPOs looked unlinked.

258
00:16:21.387 --> 00:16:25.357
And Intune's Group Policy
analytics imports XML reports

259
00:16:25.357 --> 00:16:29.360
under four megabytes, so
check sizes in the manifest.

260
00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:32.860
AD CS matters twice in a migration.

261
00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:38.095
It issues the certificates devices, domain
controllers and smart cards depend on,

262
00:16:38.095 --> 00:16:41.020
and its misconfigurations
are among the most

263
00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:44.022
direct privilege-escalation
paths in a forest.

264
00:16:44.022 --> 00:16:48.269
The ESC naming comes from
SpecterOps' 2021 research,

265
00:16:48.269 --> 00:16:54.785
which defined ESC1 through ESC8; its
tooling now covers patterns through ESC16.

266
00:16:54.785 --> 00:16:58.345
Defender for Identity's definitions
are the program's reference,

267
00:16:58.345 --> 00:17:00.785
and the assessment checks
the ones it can see.

268
00:17:00.785 --> 00:17:04.597
ESC1 is a template where the
enrollee supplies the subject,

269
00:17:04.597 --> 00:17:09.109
an authentication extended key usage
is allowed, no manager approval

270
00:17:09.109 --> 00:17:13.310
or authorized signature is required,
and broad groups can enroll.

271
00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:17.660
ESC2 is a template with the Any
Purpose usage, or none at all.

272
00:17:17.660 --> 00:17:20.713
ESC4 is a template ACL that gives a broad

273
00:17:20.713 --> 00:17:24.322
group rights such as Full
Control or Write DACL.

274
00:17:24.322 --> 00:17:30.245
ESC6 is the CA flag that lets every requester
specify the subject alternative name

275
00:17:30.245 --> 00:17:35.823
for every template; Microsoft's fix is to
clear the flag and restart the service.

276
00:17:35.823 --> 00:17:40.698
ESC7 is broad groups holding
Manage CA or Manage Certificates.

277
00:17:40.698 --> 00:17:45.347
That one needs the CA's own security
settings, which Appendix B's collector

278
00:17:45.347 --> 00:17:50.073
doesn't read; Defender for Identity
assesses it where a sensor runs on the CA.

279
00:17:50.073 --> 00:17:52.240
The correction that matters most:

280
00:17:52.240 --> 00:17:56.498
the source's ESC1 check tested for
the Digital Signature key usage.

281
00:17:56.498 --> 00:18:00.411
Almost every template has that,
so it flagged nearly everything.

282
00:18:00.411 --> 00:18:05.485
The check has to test the extended key
usage for an authentication purpose: client

283
00:18:05.485 --> 00:18:10.410
authentication, smart card logon, PKINIT
client authentication, or Any Purpose.

284
00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:13.521
And a template that passes
is a candidate only if a

285
00:18:13.521 --> 00:18:16.860
CA actually publishes it
and broad groups can enroll.

286
00:18:16.860 --> 00:18:17.860
Report only.

287
00:18:17.860 --> 00:18:20.557
Remediation belongs to the PKI owner,

288
00:18:20.557 --> 00:18:24.472
through the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 07.

289
00:18:24.472 --> 00:18:27.901
The same inventory also tells
you whether user certificates

290
00:18:27.901 --> 00:18:31.220
can move to Entra
certificate-based authentication.

291
00:18:31.970 --> 00:18:36.055
Every computer object is a
migration decision: Entra join,

292
00:18:36.055 --> 00:18:40.395
hybrid join, Azure Arc, retirement,
or keep in Active Directory.

293
00:18:40.395 --> 00:18:44.875
The inventory carries the delegation
attributes, and the source got two

294
00:18:44.875 --> 00:18:48.907
things wrong here, so classify by
what's actually set, in this order.

295
00:18:48.907 --> 00:18:53.270
Trusted for delegation set to
true is unconstrained delegation.

296
00:18:53.270 --> 00:18:58.570
It lets a service impersonate any user
who authenticates to it, to any service.

297
00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:02.875
Domain controllers are expected to
have it, so exclude them as Defender

298
00:19:02.875 --> 00:19:07.545
for Identity does; every other unconstrained
entry becomes a critical issue.

299
00:19:07.545 --> 00:19:11.096
A target list plus
trusted-to-authenticate-for-delegation

300
00:19:11.096 --> 00:19:14.357
is constrained delegation
with protocol transition.

301
00:19:14.357 --> 00:19:17.908
A target list alone is
constrained, Kerberos only.

302
00:19:17.908 --> 00:19:21.995
The source labeled protocol transition
even without a target list.

303
00:19:21.995 --> 00:19:24.632
And resource-based constrained delegation,

304
00:19:24.632 --> 00:19:28.695
which the source missed entirely,
is configured on the resource side.

305
00:19:28.695 --> 00:19:34.328
Staleness: Last Logon Date is a converted
timestamp that can lag the true last logon

306
00:19:34.328 --> 00:19:39.308
by up to two weeks, so it's good enough
for a ninety-day test, not a seven-day one.

307
00:19:39.308 --> 00:19:41.262
Pair it with Password Last Set,

308
00:19:41.262 --> 00:19:44.545
and treat a computer as
stale only when both are old.

309
00:19:44.545 --> 00:19:49.474
For users, expand the eight privileged
groups recursively: Domain Admins,

310
00:19:49.474 --> 00:19:54.807
Enterprise Admins, Schema Admins,
Administrators and the four operator groups.

311
00:19:54.807 --> 00:19:59.375
Then report orphaned admin count
accounts, using recursive membership;

312
00:19:59.375 --> 00:20:04.095
the source's direct match missed nested
members and over-reported orphans.

313
00:20:04.095 --> 00:20:06.320
Service accounts come in four kinds.

314
00:20:06.320 --> 00:20:09.177
User accounts with service
principal names are

315
00:20:09.177 --> 00:20:12.320
Kerberoastable and the
highest priority to replace.

316
00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:15.065
Group managed service
accounts span servers

317
00:20:15.065 --> 00:20:17.882
and stay the default for
multi-server services.

318
00:20:17.882 --> 00:20:21.474
Delegated managed service
accounts exist only in

319
00:20:21.474 --> 00:20:25.408
Server 2025 domains and
disable the original password.

320
00:20:25.408 --> 00:20:27.683
Two limits matter in large forests:

321
00:20:27.683 --> 00:20:33.256
a domain controller returns at most five
thousand values of a multivalued attribute

322
00:20:33.256 --> 00:20:38.410
per response, and a principal can be in
at most one thousand fifteen groups.

323
00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:42.772
Trusts are where undocumented
history usually hides.

324
00:20:42.772 --> 00:20:47.481
Each trust is a trusted domain object
in its domain's System container,

325
00:20:47.481 --> 00:20:49.673
and Get-AD-Trust returns them all.

326
00:20:49.673 --> 00:20:53.362
Decode the trust attributes from
the published bit values in

327
00:20:53.362 --> 00:20:57.197
the Active Directory technical
specification, not from memory.

328
00:20:57.197 --> 00:20:58.822
One means non-transitive.

329
00:20:58.822 --> 00:21:03.085
Two, uplevel only: Windows
2000 and later clients.

330
00:21:03.085 --> 00:21:06.910
Four, quarantined domain:
SID filtering applies.

331
00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:12.048
Eight, forest transitive: a cross-forest
trust between forest root domains.

332
00:21:12.048 --> 00:21:16.873
Hex ten, cross-organization: the
partner isn't part of your organization.

333
00:21:16.873 --> 00:21:18.985
Hex twenty, within forest.

334
00:21:18.985 --> 00:21:24.760
And hex forty, treat as external: a forest
trust filtered as if it were external.

335
00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:29.294
The specification also defines
later bits, for AES keys,

336
00:21:29.294 --> 00:21:35.897
RC4 and TGT delegation control, so record
the raw value as well as the decoded list.

337
00:21:35.897 --> 00:21:38.498
That way, bits added later aren't lost.

338
00:21:38.498 --> 00:21:42.777
Direction is relative, and access
flows the opposite way to trust:

339
00:21:42.777 --> 00:21:47.611
members of the trusted domain can
reach resources in the trusting domain.

340
00:21:47.611 --> 00:21:52.641
Seen from one domain, an inbound trust lets
this domain's users reach the partner's

341
00:21:52.641 --> 00:21:57.522
resources, and an outbound trust lets
the partner's users reach resources here.

342
00:21:57.522 --> 00:21:59.234
The source had this reversed.

343
00:21:59.234 --> 00:22:02.229
So for each trust, the
assessment states plainly

344
00:22:02.229 --> 00:22:05.373
which side's users can reach
which side's resources.

345
00:22:05.373 --> 00:22:06.623
Then rate posture.

346
00:22:06.623 --> 00:22:09.210
Selective authentication on is hardened.

347
00:22:09.210 --> 00:22:11.148
SID filtering on is standard.

348
00:22:11.148 --> 00:22:12.447
Neither is a finding.

349
00:22:12.447 --> 00:22:15.536
Verification: the source
called Test-AD-Trust,

350
00:22:15.536 --> 00:22:18.948
which isn't a cmdlet in
the ActiveDirectory module.

351
00:22:18.948 --> 00:22:24.023
Use netdom trust with the verify switch,
which checks the secure channel secrets,

352
00:22:24.023 --> 00:22:27.735
or netdom query trust with
verify for every outbound trust.

353
00:22:27.735 --> 00:22:30.288
Both need administrative credentials,

354
00:22:30.288 --> 00:22:35.472
so verification runs as its own elevated
step, outside the scheduled collection.

355
00:22:35.472 --> 00:22:40.472
Finally, a forest trust doesn't
convert into anything in Entra ID.

356
00:22:40.472 --> 00:22:44.065
It identifies a partner whose
access has to be redesigned

357
00:22:44.065 --> 00:22:47.950
with cross-tenant access
settings, which Module 4 covers.

358
00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:52.225
What makes a run evidence is the
pipeline around the collectors.

359
00:22:52.225 --> 00:22:58.434
The orchestrator runs them in the fixed
order and writes the assessment manifest:

360
00:22:58.434 --> 00:23:04.095
a new GUID for the run; the domain, host
and account; the start, end and duration;

361
00:23:04.095 --> 00:23:10.213
the staleness threshold; and every output
file with its size, time and SHA-256 hash.

362
00:23:10.213 --> 00:23:14.587
A failed step is recorded in the
manifest rather than stopping the run,

363
00:23:14.587 --> 00:23:16.925
so a partial run is visible as partial.

364
00:23:16.925 --> 00:23:19.839
Anyone holding the
repository can recompute the

365
00:23:19.839 --> 00:23:22.825
hashes and show nothing
changed after collection.

366
00:23:22.825 --> 00:23:24.850
Then one run, one commit.

367
00:23:24.850 --> 00:23:28.650
The reference design uses a
self-hosted Gitea instance.

368
00:23:28.650 --> 00:23:33.929
Each run gets its own branch, named for the
domain and timestamp, and the multi-file

369
00:23:33.929 --> 00:23:39.050
contents endpoint commits the whole run,
manifest included, as a single commit.

370
00:23:39.050 --> 00:23:43.787
The source committed one file per API
call, hundreds of commits per run.

371
00:23:43.787 --> 00:23:48.575
Issues are raised automatically for two
classes only: critical PKI patterns,

372
00:23:48.575 --> 00:23:53.213
and unconstrained delegation on accounts
that aren't domain controllers.

373
00:23:53.213 --> 00:23:58.422
Everything else goes to the planning
documents: GPO reports to the Intune plan,

374
00:23:58.422 --> 00:24:00.346
computers to the device plan,

375
00:24:00.346 --> 00:24:04.594
the PKI report to certificate-based
authentication readiness,

376
00:24:04.594 --> 00:24:09.724
trusts to the partner access plan, and
users and groups to the identity design.

377
00:24:09.724 --> 00:24:12.888
Drift: run weekly and
compare with the last run.

378
00:24:12.888 --> 00:24:15.437
A changed hash tells you
that something changed.

379
00:24:15.437 --> 00:24:19.523
To say what, compare parsed
objects by a stable key,

380
00:24:19.523 --> 00:24:22.512
a GUID or SID, and exclude timestamps.

381
00:24:22.512 --> 00:24:26.549
Schedule under the managed service
account, so no password is stored.

382
00:24:26.549 --> 00:24:29.662
Handling: the data is
a map for an attacker.

383
00:24:29.662 --> 00:24:32.182
Encrypt it, restrict the repository,

384
00:24:32.182 --> 00:24:37.725
keep runs at least twelve months and delete
after thirty-six unless a legal hold

385
00:24:37.725 --> 00:24:39.404
applies, sign the module,

386
00:24:39.404 --> 00:24:43.351
and keep the API token in a
credential store, never in a URL.

387
00:24:43.351 --> 00:24:47.630
Appendix B lists sixteen corrections
made to the source design.

388
00:24:48.380 --> 00:24:52.593
Part 2: building the Entra ID
target and migrating onto it.

389
00:24:52.593 --> 00:24:55.031
Module 3 is the target design.

390
00:24:55.031 --> 00:24:57.842
The chapter's premise
fits in one sentence.

391
00:24:57.842 --> 00:25:02.055
In Active Directory, where an object
sits decides what applies to it.

392
00:25:02.055 --> 00:25:05.855
In Entra ID, what an object
says about itself decides.

393
00:25:05.855 --> 00:25:10.430
So the target has to be built from
attributes, not copied from containers.

394
00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:16.212
The environment throughout is a Microsoft
365 Government Community Cloud tenant,

395
00:25:16.212 --> 00:25:19.815
which uses the worldwide
Microsoft Graph endpoint,

396
00:25:19.815 --> 00:25:23.418
not the separate endpoints
that GCC High and DoD use.

397
00:25:23.418 --> 00:25:27.481
Check each feature against the
government service descriptions,

398
00:25:27.481 --> 00:25:29.620
because some reach GCC later.

399
00:25:30.370 --> 00:25:35.612
Before any object moves, decompose
the AD identity model into its parts,

400
00:25:35.612 --> 00:25:37.745
because each has its own path.

401
00:25:37.745 --> 00:25:41.008
OUs do three jobs, and each
goes somewhere different.

402
00:25:41.008 --> 00:25:45.648
Group Policy scoping becomes
Conditional Access and Intune profiles,

403
00:25:45.648 --> 00:25:48.558
targeted by group
membership, not container.

404
00:25:48.558 --> 00:25:54.114
Administrative delegation becomes
administrative units, which are free to create,

405
00:25:54.114 --> 00:25:58.558
though each administrative unit
(AU)-scoped administrator needs P1.

406
00:25:58.558 --> 00:26:02.670
Logical organization becomes
dynamic groups and attributes.

407
00:26:02.670 --> 00:26:07.646
The forest maps to the tenant: one trust
boundary, no domain trusts inside it,

408
00:26:07.646 --> 00:26:12.220
and verified domains are routing
suffixes, not security boundaries.

409
00:26:12.220 --> 00:26:16.145
Trusts become B2B and
cross-tenant access settings.

410
00:26:16.145 --> 00:26:20.149
AdminSDHolder has no equivalent;
Privileged Identity Management,

411
00:26:20.149 --> 00:26:24.439
which makes assignments eligible and
time-bound, and protected actions,

412
00:26:24.439 --> 00:26:28.014
which demand an authentication
context before high-impact

413
00:26:28.014 --> 00:26:30.945
permissions are used,
together serve its purpose.

414
00:26:30.945 --> 00:26:34.895
And Schema Admins has none:
Microsoft manages the schema.

415
00:26:34.895 --> 00:26:39.457
This is the design principle: Entra
ID inverts the governance model.

416
00:26:39.457 --> 00:26:43.520
Attributes decide memberships,
and memberships decide policy.

417
00:26:43.520 --> 00:26:46.545
That's why attribute quality
gates everything after it.

418
00:26:46.545 --> 00:26:48.882
Licensing gates the governance features.

419
00:26:48.882 --> 00:26:51.045
Conditional Access needs P1.

420
00:26:51.045 --> 00:26:54.170
Dynamic groups need P1 per unique member.

421
00:26:54.170 --> 00:26:59.295
PIM, including PIM for Groups, and
risk-based Conditional Access need P2.

422
00:26:59.295 --> 00:27:04.301
As of October 2026, access reviews and
entitlement management need Entra ID

423
00:27:04.301 --> 00:27:07.083
Governance for the member users involved,

424
00:27:07.083 --> 00:27:12.645
with only some capabilities left under P2,
and Lifecycle Workflows need Governance.

425
00:27:12.645 --> 00:27:15.485
Conditional Access for
workload identities is

426
00:27:15.485 --> 00:27:18.395
a separate Workload
Identities Premium license.

427
00:27:18.395 --> 00:27:23.495
The chapters assume P2 for everyone and
Governance for the governed populations.

428
00:27:23.495 --> 00:27:29.495
One date to act on: the preview
member-of operator ends November 3, 2026.

429
00:27:29.495 --> 00:27:32.732
After that, dynamic groups,
dynamic administrative

430
00:27:32.732 --> 00:27:36.120
units and auto-assignment
policies that use it freeze.

431
00:27:36.120 --> 00:27:37.990
Replace those rules now.

432
00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:41.827
The first hybrid-period
decision is the sync engine.

433
00:27:41.827 --> 00:27:46.740
Connect Sync is the long-standing,
full-featured engine on a Windows Server.

434
00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:51.957
Cloud Sync is a lightweight agent with its
configuration in the cloud, and Microsoft

435
00:27:51.957 --> 00:27:56.652
now calls it the strategic direction:
new features are built primarily on it.

436
00:27:56.652 --> 00:27:59.171
Here's the comparison from Microsoft's

437
00:27:59.171 --> 00:28:02.240
decision guide as of its
October 2026 revision.

438
00:28:02.240 --> 00:28:05.974
Connect Sync runs on a server;
Cloud Sync runs as agents,

439
00:28:05.974 --> 00:28:09.128
which can sit on existing
domain controllers.

440
00:28:09.128 --> 00:28:11.410
Both handle connected multi-forest;

441
00:28:11.410 --> 00:28:16.203
only Cloud Sync handles disconnected
forests, which matters after mergers.

442
00:28:16.203 --> 00:28:20.654
For availability, Connect has one
active server with a staging standby;

443
00:28:20.654 --> 00:28:24.728
Cloud Sync runs multiple active
agents with automatic failover.

444
00:28:24.728 --> 00:28:29.859
Scale: Cloud Sync supports up to a hundred
fifty thousand objects per AD domain and

445
00:28:29.859 --> 00:28:33.480
groups up to fifty thousand
members; Connect has no stated

446
00:28:33.480 --> 00:28:37.253
object limit and goes to two
hundred fifty thousand members.

447
00:28:37.253 --> 00:28:42.577
Connect has advanced sync rules and cross-forest
references; Cloud Sync has neither.

448
00:28:42.577 --> 00:28:46.502
And Cloud Sync can provision
groups from Entra ID back to AD,

449
00:28:46.502 --> 00:28:49.865
and test with on-demand
provisioning; Connect can't.

450
00:28:49.865 --> 00:28:52.077
Two corrections to older guidance.

451
00:28:52.077 --> 00:28:57.385
Group Writeback version two in Connect
Sync was a preview that Microsoft

452
00:28:57.385 --> 00:29:03.041
discontinued on June 30, 2024; Cloud Sync's
group provisioning to AD replaces it.

453
00:29:03.041 --> 00:29:06.457
And Cloud Sync now handles
Exchange hybrid writeback,

454
00:29:06.457 --> 00:29:09.190
so Exchange alone no
longer forces Connect.

455
00:29:09.190 --> 00:29:12.277
The program's rule: Cloud
Sync for new deployments,

456
00:29:12.277 --> 00:29:15.590
and plan to migrate existing
Connect installations.

457
00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:20.144
Stay on Connect only where a forest
needs something Cloud Sync doesn't do:

458
00:29:20.144 --> 00:29:25.305
more than a hundred fifty thousand objects
in a domain, groups over fifty thousand,

459
00:29:25.305 --> 00:29:29.252
advanced rules, cross-forest
references, or attribute merging.

460
00:29:29.252 --> 00:29:33.487
Both engines can run in one forest
with mutually exclusive scopes,

461
00:29:33.487 --> 00:29:35.027
so you can move in stages.

462
00:29:35.027 --> 00:29:39.590
And never run two Connect servers
in active mode against one tenant.

463
00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:43.440
The second decision is where
passwords are validated.

464
00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:48.666
With password hash sync, validation happens
in the cloud against a synchronized hash

465
00:29:48.666 --> 00:29:51.758
of the password hash, with
no extra infrastructure,

466
00:29:51.758 --> 00:29:54.040
and it survives an on-premises outage.

467
00:29:54.040 --> 00:29:59.402
Pass-through authentication validates in
the cloud after an on-premises agent checks

468
00:29:59.402 --> 00:30:03.201
AD; Microsoft recommends at
least three agents in production,

469
00:30:03.201 --> 00:30:06.403
treated as tier zero, and it
doesn't survive an outage.

470
00:30:06.403 --> 00:30:11.253
Federation with AD FS validates
on-premises and needs AD FS and

471
00:30:11.253 --> 00:30:16.103
Web Application Proxy farms,
load balancers and certificates.

472
00:30:16.103 --> 00:30:19.290
Leaked-credential detection
needs password hash sync.

473
00:30:19.290 --> 00:30:23.144
Microsoft recommends enabling
it whichever method you choose:

474
00:30:23.144 --> 00:30:27.362
it's the fallback if agents or
federation fail, and organizations that

475
00:30:27.362 --> 00:30:31.653
had it on recovered from ransomware
outages in hours rather than weeks.

476
00:30:31.653 --> 00:30:33.477
The failover is manual.

477
00:30:33.477 --> 00:30:38.085
So the program's position is password
hash sync preferred everywhere,

478
00:30:38.085 --> 00:30:41.978
pass-through transitional, and
AD FS a decommission target.

479
00:30:41.978 --> 00:30:43.240
Then, matching.

480
00:30:43.240 --> 00:30:47.427
When sync adds an object that
might already exist in the cloud,

481
00:30:47.427 --> 00:30:50.876
Entra ID tries a hard match
first: the source anchor,

482
00:30:50.876 --> 00:30:55.802
by default the consistency GUID,
against the cloud object's immutable ID.

483
00:30:55.802 --> 00:30:59.896
Without one, a soft match on
UPN or primary SMTP address,

484
00:30:59.896 --> 00:31:04.353
which only works if the cloud
object has no immutable ID yet.

485
00:31:04.353 --> 00:31:07.813
Either one overwrites the
cloud object's attributes,

486
00:31:07.813 --> 00:31:10.802
including the password
hash, so clean AD first.

487
00:31:10.802 --> 00:31:14.595
Two tenant settings block soft
match and hard-match takeover;

488
00:31:14.595 --> 00:31:19.353
Microsoft advises leaving both blocked
except in a planned matching window.

489
00:31:19.353 --> 00:31:24.776
And since July 1, 2026, Entra ID blocks
a hard match onto a cloud account that

490
00:31:24.776 --> 00:31:28.155
already has an on-premises
object identifier,

491
00:31:28.155 --> 00:31:32.690
or holds or is eligible for a
privileged role, for both engines.

492
00:31:32.690 --> 00:31:36.580
Don't plan to take over cloud
administrator accounts by sync.

493
00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:41.630
Groups and service accounts each get
one recorded decision per object.

494
00:31:41.630 --> 00:31:45.494
For groups, the AGDLP model
doesn't map one to one,

495
00:31:45.494 --> 00:31:48.367
because Entra ID has no group scope.

496
00:31:48.367 --> 00:31:51.955
So every AD security group
gets one of five decisions.

497
00:31:51.955 --> 00:31:54.822
Used only for on-premises resource ACLs:

498
00:31:54.822 --> 00:31:59.080
keep it in AD and don't sync it,
since a cloud copy adds nothing.

499
00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:02.543
Used for both on-premises
and cloud: synchronize it,

500
00:32:02.543 --> 00:32:06.418
with AD as the source of
authority during the transition.

501
00:32:06.418 --> 00:32:10.254
Used only for cloud resources:
convert it to cloud-only.

502
00:32:10.254 --> 00:32:15.067
Membership follows attributes exactly:
convert it to a dynamic group.

503
00:32:15.067 --> 00:32:18.318
No members or no assignments: retire it.

504
00:32:18.318 --> 00:32:22.511
Where a cloud-only group still has
to guard a Kerberos application,

505
00:32:22.511 --> 00:32:26.018
Cloud Sync provisions it back
to AD as a universal group.

506
00:32:26.018 --> 00:32:28.979
Nesting works for fewer
things in Entra ID.

507
00:32:28.979 --> 00:32:33.439
Conditional Access follows nested
members, but group-based application

508
00:32:33.439 --> 00:32:37.605
assignment and group-based licensing
don't cascade to nested groups.

509
00:32:37.605 --> 00:32:42.143
So flatten deep AGDLP chains to
direct membership or a dynamic rule,

510
00:32:42.143 --> 00:32:44.980
and record the mapping
for the audit trail.

511
00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:49.080
For service accounts, the rule
is to eliminate stored secrets.

512
00:32:49.080 --> 00:32:54.930
A traditional account that calls Microsoft
365 or other Entra-protected APIs becomes

513
00:32:54.930 --> 00:32:58.261
an application registration
with a certificate,

514
00:32:58.261 --> 00:33:02.242
or a federated credential where
the host has an OIDC issuer.

515
00:33:02.242 --> 00:33:04.915
One whose workload runs
on an Azure resource

516
00:33:04.915 --> 00:33:08.093
becomes a managed identity,
user-assigned preferred.

517
00:33:08.093 --> 00:33:11.861
On-premises Kerberos workloads,
and standalone MSAs,

518
00:33:11.861 --> 00:33:14.517
go to a group managed service account.

519
00:33:14.517 --> 00:33:19.742
A gMSA whose workload moves to the cloud
goes to workload identity federation.

520
00:33:19.742 --> 00:33:23.704
A federated credential has
three working parts: the issuer,

521
00:33:23.704 --> 00:33:28.231
a subject that must match exactly
with no wildcards, and an audience.

522
00:33:28.231 --> 00:33:30.783
An application holds at most twenty,

523
00:33:30.783 --> 00:33:35.292
and a wrong subject fails only at
token exchange, so test each one.

524
00:33:35.292 --> 00:33:40.580
Every application gets two human owners,
and no client secrets in production.

525
00:33:41.330 --> 00:33:46.480
Privileged access has one core rule: no
standing access to privileged roles.

526
00:33:46.480 --> 00:33:50.980
Every privileged assignment is
eligible and activated just in time.

527
00:33:50.980 --> 00:33:54.880
The only permanent active assignments
are the two emergency accounts.

528
00:33:54.880 --> 00:33:59.430
The mapping from AD groups is the
program's design, not an equivalence.

529
00:33:59.430 --> 00:34:04.515
Domain Admins maps to Global
Administrator, held by very few; Microsoft

530
00:34:04.515 --> 00:34:10.118
recommends fewer than five, and most
Domain Admin duties map to narrower roles.

531
00:34:10.118 --> 00:34:13.758
Enterprise Admins maps to
Privileged Role Administrator,

532
00:34:13.758 --> 00:34:15.467
with its own approval chain.

533
00:34:15.467 --> 00:34:20.185
Account Operators becomes User Administrator
scoped to administrative units;

534
00:34:20.185 --> 00:34:23.423
Conditional Access can't
target AU-scoped assignments,

535
00:34:23.423 --> 00:34:26.943
so put those administrators in
a group for policy targeting.

536
00:34:26.943 --> 00:34:30.882
Server Operators moves to Intune
or Azure role-based access,

537
00:34:30.882 --> 00:34:33.797
Backup Operators to
service-specific roles,

538
00:34:33.797 --> 00:34:38.918
and Group Policy Creator Owners to the
Intune Policy and Profile Manager role.

539
00:34:38.918 --> 00:34:43.633
The PIM standards: Global Administrator
and Privileged Role Administrator

540
00:34:43.633 --> 00:34:48.568
activate for at most four hours, with
approval, justification and a ticket number.

541
00:34:48.568 --> 00:34:51.842
Security Administrator gets
eight hours with approval.

542
00:34:51.842 --> 00:34:55.140
Most other admin roles get
eight hours without approval,

543
00:34:55.140 --> 00:34:56.754
and the reader roles twelve.

544
00:34:56.754 --> 00:35:01.917
Eligibility expires within twelve months
and renews through an access review.

545
00:35:01.917 --> 00:35:06.351
And because PIM's own MFA setting can
be satisfied by an earlier sign-in,

546
00:35:06.351 --> 00:35:10.707
the program uses PIM's authentication
context option to force fresh,

547
00:35:10.707 --> 00:35:13.993
phishing-resistant
authentication at activation.

548
00:35:13.993 --> 00:35:18.280
Configure named approvers outside
the requester's chain, because with

549
00:35:18.280 --> 00:35:22.568
none configured, PIM falls back to
active admins and can lock the tenant.

550
00:35:22.568 --> 00:35:26.911
Emergency access: two cloud-only
accounts in the onmicrosoft domain,

551
00:35:26.911 --> 00:35:31.256
permanent active Global Administrator,
on a passkey security key or

552
00:35:31.256 --> 00:35:35.829
certificate-based authentication,
each credential stored separately.

553
00:35:35.829 --> 00:35:40.656
Microsoft's mandatory MFA for
admin portals applies to them too,

554
00:35:40.656 --> 00:35:43.205
so a password in a safe isn't enough.

555
00:35:43.205 --> 00:35:46.369
Exclude them from every
restrictive policy,

556
00:35:46.369 --> 00:35:51.080
alert on every sign-in, and test
them at least every ninety days.

557
00:35:51.830 --> 00:35:55.586
Conditional Access replaces the
logon restrictions and network

558
00:35:55.586 --> 00:35:59.205
controls that Active Directory
enforced through Group Policy.

559
00:35:59.205 --> 00:36:04.893
The program organizes it around personas:
populations with distinct needs and risk.

560
00:36:04.893 --> 00:36:09.805
Internals are employees and contractors,
identified by the worker type code.

561
00:36:09.805 --> 00:36:13.711
Admins are users eligible for
or holding privileged roles,

562
00:36:13.711 --> 00:36:18.972
identified by account type or a group of
PIM-eligible users; target them by group,

563
00:36:18.972 --> 00:36:23.197
because role targeting supports
built-in directory roles only,

564
00:36:23.197 --> 00:36:25.030
not AU-scoped or custom ones.

565
00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:30.183
Externals are guests from partner organizations,
Guests are ad hoc external users,

566
00:36:30.183 --> 00:36:33.768
Service accounts are
single-tenant workload identities,

567
00:36:33.768 --> 00:36:36.830
and Break-glass is the
dedicated exclusion group.

568
00:36:36.830 --> 00:36:41.855
The baseline set runs
from CA001 through CA017.

569
00:36:41.855 --> 00:36:43.067
A few anchors.

570
00:36:43.067 --> 00:36:47.893
CA001 blocks legacy
authentication for all users.

571
00:36:47.893 --> 00:36:53.301
CA003 requires the phishing-resistant
authentication strength for admins,

572
00:36:53.301 --> 00:36:55.667
with a four-hour sign-in frequency.

573
00:36:55.667 --> 00:37:02.343
CA006 requires MFA for internals,
phishing-resistant as the end state.

574
00:37:02.343 --> 00:37:06.892
CA008 blocks internals
outside trusted countries.

575
00:37:06.892 --> 00:37:13.448
CA013 uses Microsoft's newer require risk
remediation control for high user risk;

576
00:37:13.448 --> 00:37:19.330
it isn't supported for guests, so CA012
blocks risky guest sign-ins instead.

577
00:37:19.330 --> 00:37:24.418
CA015 blocks workload identities
outside trusted networks.

578
00:37:24.418 --> 00:37:29.630
CA002, the old break-glass grant
policy, is retired: a policy can't

579
00:37:29.630 --> 00:37:35.743
create an exemption; the exclusion on
every other policy is the exemption.

580
00:37:35.743 --> 00:37:41.405
Appendix C carries Graph JSON templates,
all created in report-only state.

581
00:37:41.405 --> 00:37:47.249
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 04's library is authoritative

582
00:37:47.249 --> 00:37:53.093
and adds CA018 through CA038
for the CISA BOD 25-01 baseline.

583
00:37:53.093 --> 00:37:58.544
Authentication strengths name what's
accepted: phishing-resistant MFA is Windows

584
00:37:58.544 --> 00:38:04.392
Hello for Business, FIDO2 passkeys, or
multifactor certificate-based authentication.

585
00:38:04.392 --> 00:38:08.805
Authenticator push and phone sign-in
aren't phishing-resistant on their own.

586
00:38:08.805 --> 00:38:13.846
Rollout has three stages: report-only
for two to four weeks, a two-week pilot,

587
00:38:13.846 --> 00:38:18.805
then phased waves over four to eight
weeks, each behind a monitoring gate.

588
00:38:18.805 --> 00:38:22.318
And never switch an all-users
policy straight from off to on.

589
00:38:22.318 --> 00:38:27.860
Confirm the break-glass exclusion, and that
both emergency accounts still sign in.

590
00:38:28.610 --> 00:38:32.310
Module 4: migrating,
governing, and proving it.

591
00:38:32.310 --> 00:38:34.500
Chapter 10 states the finish line:

592
00:38:34.500 --> 00:38:37.785
the migration ends when
governance runs on its own.

593
00:38:37.785 --> 00:38:41.931
Joiners, movers and leavers flow
from the HR record without a ticket,

594
00:38:41.931 --> 00:38:46.374
every privileged session starts with
a phishing-resistant credential,

595
00:38:46.374 --> 00:38:49.410
and drift shows up in a diff
instead of an incident.

596
00:38:49.410 --> 00:38:51.507
We'll cover the governance machinery,

597
00:38:51.507 --> 00:38:55.700
the authentication roadmap and the
eleven-phase playbook from Chapter 10,

598
00:38:55.700 --> 00:38:59.961
then Chapter 11's reference deployment,
which runs the whole sequence on one

599
00:38:59.961 --> 00:39:04.910
representative two-forest estate and shows
what the assessor receives at the end.

600
00:39:05.660 --> 00:39:09.045
Governance in Active Directory
was mostly a ticket queue:

601
00:39:09.045 --> 00:39:13.135
someone asked for membership, someone
added it, and nobody took it away.

602
00:39:13.135 --> 00:39:15.323
The target replaces that with a loop.

603
00:39:15.323 --> 00:39:18.986
HR-driven provisioning creates
and updates the account.

604
00:39:18.986 --> 00:39:23.235
Lifecycle Workflows run the
joiner, mover and leaver tasks.

605
00:39:23.235 --> 00:39:26.373
Access packages grant
access with an expiry.

606
00:39:26.373 --> 00:39:29.747
And access reviews remove
what's no longer justified.

607
00:39:29.747 --> 00:39:34.534
Lifecycle Workflows act on accounts that
already exist; they don't create them.

608
00:39:34.534 --> 00:39:38.485
The time-based triggers key on
the native employee hire date and

609
00:39:38.485 --> 00:39:42.585
leave date attributes, with an offset
of up to a hundred eighty days.

610
00:39:42.585 --> 00:39:47.322
For synced users, those dates must
be synced into Entra ID first.

611
00:39:47.322 --> 00:39:52.541
Pre-hire runs seven days before the hire
date: confirm the account exists, disabled;

612
00:39:52.541 --> 00:39:55.073
request the onboarding access package;

613
00:39:55.073 --> 00:39:58.911
and generate a Temporary Access
Pass emailed to the manager,

614
00:39:58.911 --> 00:40:03.285
so the new hire registers a
phishing-resistant credential on day one.

615
00:40:03.285 --> 00:40:07.764
Joiner runs on the day: enable the
account, assign licenses by group,

616
00:40:07.764 --> 00:40:11.510
send the welcome email,
request the department package.

617
00:40:11.510 --> 00:40:16.306
Mover fires on a change to department,
division code or position category:

618
00:40:16.306 --> 00:40:19.923
remove the old department's
package, request the new one,

619
00:40:19.923 --> 00:40:25.348
let dynamic groups and a dynamic administrative
unit adjust, and review what's left.

620
00:40:25.348 --> 00:40:28.554
Leaver runs on the leave
date: disable the account,

621
00:40:28.554 --> 00:40:30.823
revoke refresh and session tokens,

622
00:40:30.823 --> 00:40:35.595
remove access packages and then licenses
after the mailbox is converted,

623
00:40:35.595 --> 00:40:39.585
remove group memberships at
thirty days and delete at ninety.

624
00:40:39.585 --> 00:40:40.748
Two cautions.

625
00:40:40.748 --> 00:40:44.521
The delete task doesn't support
users holding Entra roles or

626
00:40:44.521 --> 00:40:48.898
role-assignable group membership,
so remove privileged access first.

627
00:40:48.898 --> 00:40:51.776
And a synced user
deleted only in the cloud

628
00:40:51.776 --> 00:40:54.572
comes back on the next
sync; delete it in AD.

629
00:40:54.572 --> 00:40:56.160
Reviews close the loop.

630
00:40:56.160 --> 00:41:01.067
PIM-eligible role assignments are
reviewed quarterly by the security team,

631
00:41:01.067 --> 00:41:04.883
not by self-review, which leaves
role-assignable groups out;

632
00:41:04.883 --> 00:41:08.310
that review is the AC-6(7) evidence.

633
00:41:08.310 --> 00:41:13.300
Sensitive groups go quarterly to
owners, guests quarterly to sponsors.

634
00:41:14.050 --> 00:41:17.775
Authentication modernization starts
from the federal requirement.

635
00:41:17.775 --> 00:41:24.140
OMB M-22-09 requires phishing-resistant
MFA for staff, contractors and partners,

636
00:41:24.140 --> 00:41:29.597
and requires agencies to stop supporting
methods that fail to resist phishing,

637
00:41:29.597 --> 00:41:32.738
such as SMS and voice
codes, for routine access.

638
00:41:32.738 --> 00:41:35.300
It names PIV as the simplest route.

639
00:41:35.300 --> 00:41:39.348
Its targets were set for
the end of fiscal year 2024,

640
00:41:39.348 --> 00:41:44.263
so as of October 2026 these are
requirements to meet, not goals.

641
00:41:44.263 --> 00:41:46.012
That reorders the roadmap.

642
00:41:46.012 --> 00:41:48.763
Older plans put smart-card sign-in last.

643
00:41:48.763 --> 00:41:52.411
But a federal workforce already
carries PIV or CAC cards,

644
00:41:52.411 --> 00:41:57.534
so Entra certificate-based authentication
is the fastest path: the credential

645
00:41:57.534 --> 00:42:02.501
is issued, and it works directly against
Entra ID without a federation server.

646
00:42:02.501 --> 00:42:07.689
Phase one, privileged accounts: FIDO2
passkeys with attestation enforced,

647
00:42:07.689 --> 00:42:11.663
plus PIV or CAC through
certificate-based authentication

648
00:42:11.663 --> 00:42:14.825
where the admin holds a
card, in four to six weeks.

649
00:42:14.825 --> 00:42:19.950
Phase two, every PIV and CAC holder
through certificate-based authentication.

650
00:42:19.950 --> 00:42:24.500
Phase three, managed Windows devices
on Windows Hello for Business.

651
00:42:24.500 --> 00:42:30.088
Phase four, everyone else, including mobile,
on passkeys in Microsoft Authenticator.

652
00:42:30.088 --> 00:42:32.813
Those last three are eight
to twelve weeks each,

653
00:42:32.813 --> 00:42:36.726
and all four methods meet the
built-in phishing-resistant strength.

654
00:42:36.726 --> 00:42:40.642
For NIST control IA-2(6), separate device,

655
00:42:40.642 --> 00:42:45.392
the program counts PIV cards and
FIDO2 keys; Windows Hello lives in

656
00:42:45.392 --> 00:42:50.225
the device gaining access, so it
isn't counted for privileged accounts.

657
00:42:50.225 --> 00:42:54.950
The authentication methods policy:
SMS and voice off for staff.

658
00:42:54.950 --> 00:42:58.862
Temporary Access Pass is one-time
use with a one-hour life,

659
00:42:58.862 --> 00:43:01.525
against Microsoft's multi-use default.

660
00:43:01.525 --> 00:43:06.050
Passkey registration needs
MFA in the last five minutes.

661
00:43:06.050 --> 00:43:07.650
Then the legacy cutoff.

662
00:43:07.650 --> 00:43:10.913
Discover remaining legacy
sign-ins in the logs.

663
00:43:10.913 --> 00:43:15.887
Remediate onto OAuth, the Graph
mail APIs, or a supported relay.

664
00:43:15.887 --> 00:43:18.250
Block with CA001.

665
00:43:18.250 --> 00:43:23.520
And monitor for thirty days until there
are zero successful legacy sign-ins.

666
00:43:24.270 --> 00:43:28.020
The migration runs in eleven
phases, zero through ten.

667
00:43:28.020 --> 00:43:31.373
Each has prerequisites,
steps, validation criteria,

668
00:43:31.373 --> 00:43:34.336
a rollback procedure
and a duration estimate,

669
00:43:34.336 --> 00:43:39.170
and a phase starts only when the previous
one's validation criteria are met.

670
00:43:39.170 --> 00:43:41.679
The durations are planning estimates,

671
00:43:41.679 --> 00:43:45.520
not measured results; the total
runs forty to seventy weeks.

672
00:43:45.520 --> 00:43:50.764
Phase zero is assessment: the forest
and identity inventories in Git, a UPN

673
00:43:50.764 --> 00:43:54.632
remediation plan, a recorded
decision for every group,

674
00:43:54.632 --> 00:43:57.383
and every service account classified.

675
00:43:57.383 --> 00:44:02.841
Phase one deploys sync: at least two Cloud
Sync agents, or Connect where required,

676
00:44:02.841 --> 00:44:04.329
password hash sync on,

677
00:44:04.329 --> 00:44:08.795
validated by object counts within
one percent and no sync errors.

678
00:44:08.795 --> 00:44:13.157
Rollback removes objects from
scope, recoverable for thirty days.

679
00:44:13.157 --> 00:44:17.755
Phase two sets the sign-in method and
puts every Conditional Access policy

680
00:44:17.755 --> 00:44:21.908
in report-only, with the emergency
account sign-in tested and logged.

681
00:44:21.908 --> 00:44:27.421
Phase three migrates groups, with dynamic
membership validated within two percent.

682
00:44:27.421 --> 00:44:31.054
Phase four modernizes service
accounts, high-risk first,

683
00:44:31.054 --> 00:44:34.920
running old and new credentials
in parallel for thirty days.

684
00:44:34.920 --> 00:44:36.466
Phase five rolls out PIM:

685
00:44:36.466 --> 00:44:40.795
zero permanent active assignments
except the emergency accounts.

686
00:44:40.795 --> 00:44:45.408
Phase six enforces Conditional
Access in waves, but only after MFA

687
00:44:45.408 --> 00:44:50.357
registration passes ninety-five
percent in each targeted population.

688
00:44:50.357 --> 00:44:53.175
Phase seven is the
phishing-resistant roadmap,

689
00:44:53.175 --> 00:44:58.520
validated by every privileged account on
FIDO2 or certificate-based authentication,

690
00:44:58.520 --> 00:45:02.204
over eighty percent of managed-device
users on Windows Hello,

691
00:45:02.204 --> 00:45:06.321
and over seventy percent of all
users on a phishing-resistant method.

692
00:45:06.321 --> 00:45:09.657
Phase eight cuts off legacy
authentication after

693
00:45:09.657 --> 00:45:12.995
thirty consecutive days
with zero legacy sign-ins.

694
00:45:12.995 --> 00:45:16.703
Phase nine decommissions
federation, where it exists:

695
00:45:16.703 --> 00:45:20.658
staged rollout moves users to
password hash sync in waves,

696
00:45:20.658 --> 00:45:25.026
then each domain is converted to
managed with Graph PowerShell,

697
00:45:25.026 --> 00:45:30.382
never the retired MSOnline cmdlet, and
AD FS stays recoverable for ninety days.

698
00:45:30.382 --> 00:45:34.670
Phase ten is steady-state governance:
workflows, access packages,

699
00:45:34.670 --> 00:45:40.430
reviews and drift detection running, and
the operations runbook committed to Git.

700
00:45:41.180 --> 00:45:45.792
Proving it starts with logs, because
Entra ID keeps them only briefly.

701
00:45:45.792 --> 00:45:51.755
Sign-in and audit logs are kept seven days
on the free tier and thirty on P1 or P2.

702
00:45:51.755 --> 00:45:55.405
Risky sign-ins reach
ninety days only with P2.

703
00:45:55.405 --> 00:46:00.043
Microsoft Graph activity logs aren't
retained at all; they're export only.

704
00:46:00.043 --> 00:46:03.030
And upgrading a license
doesn't recover older data.

705
00:46:03.030 --> 00:46:06.921
So stream every category to
a Log Analytics workspace and

706
00:46:06.921 --> 00:46:10.730
keep sign-in and audit logs
three hundred sixty-five days.

707
00:46:10.730 --> 00:46:15.023
On top of that, scheduled detections:
stale accounts over ninety days;

708
00:46:15.023 --> 00:46:18.639
PIM activation between ten at
night and six in the morning,

709
00:46:18.639 --> 00:46:23.911
after converting from UTC; interactive
sign-ins with no Conditional Access applied;

710
00:46:23.911 --> 00:46:29.259
application credentials expiring within
thirty days; any emergency account sign-in,

711
00:46:29.259 --> 00:46:34.230
which is critical; and more than fifty
membership changes to one group in an hour.

712
00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:39.564
For compliance, Compliance Manager has
templates for NIST 800-53 Revision 5 and

713
00:46:39.564 --> 00:46:43.604
FedRAMP Moderate, but they don't
produce indicator evidence,

714
00:46:43.604 --> 00:46:45.867
so treat them as a readiness check.

715
00:46:45.867 --> 00:46:49.907
Under FedRAMP 20x's
Consolidated Rules for 2026,

716
00:46:49.907 --> 00:46:53.305
the controls map to Key
Security Indicators.

717
00:46:53.305 --> 00:46:56.882
Account management maps to
automating account management,

718
00:46:56.882 --> 00:46:59.243
evidenced by the workflows and reviews.

719
00:46:59.243 --> 00:47:02.737
Least privilege maps to
ensuring least privilege and

720
00:47:02.737 --> 00:47:06.154
authorizing just-in-time,
evidenced by groups and PIM.

721
00:47:06.154 --> 00:47:09.867
Authentication maps to
adopting passwordless methods.

722
00:47:09.867 --> 00:47:14.249
Non-user authentication maps to
securing non-user authentication,

723
00:47:14.249 --> 00:47:15.892
the service account work.

724
00:47:15.892 --> 00:47:20.193
And event logging maps to logging
event types and reviewing logs.

725
00:47:20.193 --> 00:47:21.605
Drift lives in Git.

726
00:47:21.605 --> 00:47:26.012
The identity assessment commits its
JSON to an identity baseline branch,

727
00:47:26.012 --> 00:47:29.841
a weekly scan on Sunday at two in
the morning compares against it,

728
00:47:29.841 --> 00:47:32.080
and drift opens an issue with the diff.

729
00:47:32.080 --> 00:47:36.617
The rule: close each finding at the
source, never by editing the copy.

730
00:47:36.617 --> 00:47:41.931
Be clear about maturity: the comparison
covers user additions and removals today;

731
00:47:41.931 --> 00:47:45.650
attribute, membership and
policy diffs are planned.

732
00:47:46.400 --> 00:47:50.163
Chapter 11 puts it together
on one representative estate.

733
00:47:50.163 --> 00:47:53.670
It's a framing pattern, not a
report on a real engagement:

734
00:47:53.670 --> 00:47:58.737
the agency is a composite and the counts
are illustrative orders of magnitude.

735
00:47:58.737 --> 00:48:03.778
The archetype has about fifty thousand
users in two forests, one inherited

736
00:48:03.778 --> 00:48:09.062
from an absorbed component, joined by a
forest trust nobody documented or owns.

737
00:48:09.062 --> 00:48:13.102
About seventy thousand computer
accounts, twelve thousand groups,

738
00:48:13.102 --> 00:48:17.574
several thousand service accounts,
and about ninety SQL Server instances.

739
00:48:17.574 --> 00:48:22.013
The destination is Microsoft 365 GCC.

740
00:48:22.013 --> 00:48:24.738
Each phase ends with a
signed readiness bundle,

741
00:48:24.738 --> 00:48:27.463
and the next phase starts
only from that bundle.

742
00:48:27.463 --> 00:48:32.887
Discover: the read-only survey of both
forests, plus the service account scan.

743
00:48:32.887 --> 00:48:35.599
About four hundred accounts carry SPNs;

744
00:48:35.599 --> 00:48:38.638
about sixty rate critical
and a hundred high.

745
00:48:38.638 --> 00:48:43.152
Every SQL Server SPN is captured
with a pre-migration tag,

746
00:48:43.152 --> 00:48:47.763
because a missing or wrong SPN
silently falls back to NTLM.

747
00:48:47.763 --> 00:48:50.259
About ten instances raise findings because

748
00:48:50.259 --> 00:48:52.688
their service accounts
can't be attributed.

749
00:48:52.688 --> 00:48:57.263
Normalize: both forests have an
Operations OU holding different people.

750
00:48:57.263 --> 00:49:00.345
The agency builds an
organizational registry and

751
00:49:00.345 --> 00:49:03.574
resolves the collision there,
not by renaming groups.

752
00:49:03.574 --> 00:49:08.056
About two thousand users and several
hundred service accounts get findings,

753
00:49:08.056 --> 00:49:11.137
resolved by the governance
team, never auto-assigned.

754
00:49:11.137 --> 00:49:14.224
Map: an adapter contract for each surface.

755
00:49:14.224 --> 00:49:15.588
Four carry the risk.

756
00:49:15.588 --> 00:49:19.268
Sync, where cross-forest manager
chains decide the engine,

757
00:49:19.268 --> 00:49:22.949
since Cloud Sync doesn't
support cross-forest references.

758
00:49:22.949 --> 00:49:26.466
PKI and RADIUS, which move in lockstep

759
00:49:26.466 --> 00:49:30.550
because 802.1X EAP-TLS
depends on the same CA.

760
00:49:30.550 --> 00:49:34.962
And SQL Server, whose Entra
authentication needs SQL Server

761
00:49:34.962 --> 00:49:39.638
2022 or later and isn't supported
on failover cluster instances.

762
00:49:39.638 --> 00:49:43.437
Migrate: every operation is
a provenance-chained record.

763
00:49:43.437 --> 00:49:46.411
About three hundred drift findings appear;

764
00:49:46.411 --> 00:49:51.201
one P1 stops a SQL Server migration
until a role assignment is reduced.

765
00:49:51.201 --> 00:49:57.620
Validate compares the SPN inventories;
about three P1 findings are re-registered.

766
00:49:58.370 --> 00:50:00.283
Last, what all this produces.

767
00:50:00.283 --> 00:50:04.745
During migration the program
classifies drift into five classes.

768
00:50:04.745 --> 00:50:09.657
Schema drift: an attribute encoding that
doesn't match the current schema version.

769
00:50:09.657 --> 00:50:12.122
Provenance drift: a state change with no

770
00:50:12.122 --> 00:50:15.183
provenance event in the
chain, which halts the run.

771
00:50:15.183 --> 00:50:20.133
Semantic drift: a value slipping back
into a deprecated naming pattern.

772
00:50:20.133 --> 00:50:23.327
Authorization drift: a
Conditional Access policy that

773
00:50:23.327 --> 00:50:26.869
contradicts the assurance level
recorded for the principal,.

774
00:50:26.869 --> 00:50:31.932
And identity drift: a principal in the
tenant with no counterpart in the registry.

775
00:50:31.932 --> 00:50:33.532
Be honest about maturity.

776
00:50:33.532 --> 00:50:37.707
Schema and provenance detection
are shipped, semantic is partial,

777
00:50:37.707 --> 00:50:41.426
and authorization and identity
detection are design-only;

778
00:50:41.426 --> 00:50:45.145
today, people reviewing the
assessment modules find those.

779
00:50:45.145 --> 00:50:49.544
From Phase 2 on, the agency can
produce an evidence bundle at any time:

780
00:50:49.544 --> 00:50:51.980
each phase's signed readiness bundle,

781
00:50:51.980 --> 00:50:55.672
both phase-tagged SPN
inventories and their comparison,

782
00:50:55.672 --> 00:50:58.971
the provenance chain for
every adapter operation,

783
00:50:58.971 --> 00:51:03.056
every drift finding with its
remediation, and the GCC boundary

784
00:51:03.056 --> 00:51:07.770
finding with its compensating controls
and when each was last verified.

785
00:51:07.770 --> 00:51:09.133
Who reads it matters.

786
00:51:09.133 --> 00:51:11.833
FedRAMP authorizes
cloud service offerings,

787
00:51:11.833 --> 00:51:15.457
and third-party assessment
organizations assess providers.

788
00:51:15.457 --> 00:51:20.858
The agency's own system is authorized
by its authorizing official under the

789
00:51:20.858 --> 00:51:26.258
Risk Management Framework, SP 800-37,
which supports ongoing authorization.

790
00:51:26.258 --> 00:51:31.203
So the bundle goes to the security control
assessor and the authorizing official,

791
00:51:31.203 --> 00:51:34.621
as it happens, not assembled
over most of a year afterward.

792
00:51:34.621 --> 00:51:36.533
And it belongs to the agency.

793
00:51:36.533 --> 00:51:39.758
When the integrator leaves,
the registry, schemas,

794
00:51:39.758 --> 00:51:43.770
findings and evidence stay for
the next authorization cycle.

795
00:51:43.770 --> 00:51:48.148
So, from Monday: start read-only
discovery with a pre-flight;

796
00:51:48.148 --> 00:51:51.615
turn the gaps into a
narrow, time-bound request;

797
00:51:51.615 --> 00:51:56.358
make every run evidence; and gate
every phase on its validation.

798
00:51:56.358 --> 00:52:01.907
Chapters 07 through 11 and the appendices
carry the full detail and the code.

799
00:52:01.907 --> 00:52:06.437
The Federal Modernization Workbook's
Book 03, HR-Driven Identity,

800
00:52:06.437 --> 00:52:09.708
picks up where the
attributes should come from.

801
00:52:09.708 --> 00:52:10.540
Thank you.
